course: everyday
lesson: 266
title: Walking on the pavement and crossing roads
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The pavement (the sidewalk in the USA and Canada) is shared by everyone, and the road beside it belongs to fast, heavy cars that need many metres to stop.
explain: So you never run into the road: you stop at the kerb, look and listen, and walk across, at a crossing, a crosswalk or a corner where you can.
explain: Drivers cannot always see you: a car reversing out of a driveway may not see a child behind it, and a car in the next lane may not see you behind a car that has stopped.
explain: A boy in Leeds walks his little brother to the shop holding his hand, on the side away from the traffic; when a car backs out of a driveway, both stop and wait until it has gone.
fact: A small child next to a busy road is held by the hand and kept on the side away from the traffic, because small children can dart into the road.
fact: When a car comes out of a driveway across the pavement, you stop and let it pass, because the driver may not see a child behind it.
fact: Ball games belong in a park, a yard or a playground, not on the pavement, because a ball can roll into the road and a child may chase it without looking.
fact: A dog tied up alone outside a shop may be nervous and snap, so you do not pat it without its owner.
fact: When it rains, you stand back from the kerb, because passing cars splash water from the puddles at the edge of the road.
fact: On a narrow pavement friends walk in a line and step aside for prams, wheelchairs, canes and walking frames, and pass slowly and politely, saying excuse me.
fact: You never run across a road: you stop, look and walk, because a running child can trip and drivers cannot stop in time.
fact: At a crosswalk you wait for the cars to stop and make eye contact with the drivers, because some drivers do not yield (let you go first).
fact: When one car stops for you, you check that every other lane has stopped too before you pass in front of it.
fact: A school crossing guard, also called a lollipop man or lollipop lady, stops the traffic, and you cross only when they signal you.
fact: If you drop something in the road while crossing, you leave it and finish crossing, then get it later when it is safe or ask an adult.
fact: Even on a one-way street you look both ways, because bikes, scooters and some buses may come the other way.
fact: A car going at speed needs many metres to stop, even when the driver has seen you.
fact: In many places in the USA, crossing in the middle of a block (jaywalking) is against local rules and more dangerous than crossing at a corner or crosswalk.
exercise: You walk your little brother along a busy road. What do you do? | Hold his hand and keep him away from the traffic side | Let him walk on the kerb edge so he can see the cars | Let him run ahead so he gets there first | Walk behind him and call out loudly whenever he goes near the road
exercise: A car starts reversing out of a driveway across the pavement in front of you. What do you do? | Stop and let it pass before walking on | Run past quickly before it comes out of the drive | Walk on, because the pavement belongs to walkers | Knock on the car window to warn the driver
exercise: Why should you be careful of cars reversing out of driveways? | The driver may not see a small child behind the car | Reversing cars always drive very fast | Cars cannot reverse onto the pavement | Drivers always sound the horn first
exercise: Where is the best place to play ball games? | In a park, a yard or a playground | On the pavement next to the road | In a quiet car park between cars | On the edge of a road with little traffic
exercise: A dog is tied up alone outside a shop. What should you do? | Leave it alone and not pat it without its owner | Untie it and walk it round the block while the owner shops | Pat it gently so it feels less lonely | Give it some of your sandwich
exercise: It is raining and there is a big puddle at the kerb. Where do you stand? | Back from the kerb, so passing cars do not splash you | Right at the kerb, so you can cross quickly when there is a gap | In the puddle, because you are already wet | On the road side of the puddle
exercise: Three friends walk side by side on a narrow pavement and a woman with a pram comes. What should they do? | Walk in a line and step aside to let her pass | Keep walking side by side; she can go round them | Make her step out into the road to get past them | Stop in the middle and chat until she waits for them to finish
exercise: An old man walks very slowly in front of you with a walking frame. What do you do? | Be patient and pass politely when there is room | Squeeze past quickly so you are not late | Walk close behind him to hurry him up | Overtake him by stepping into the road
exercise: Your friend is on the other side of the road. What do you do? | Stop, look and walk across safely | Run across quickly so you do not miss them | Shout for them to run across to you quickly | Cross between parked cars to save time
exercise: You are at a crosswalk in the USA. What do you do before crossing? | Wait for the cars to stop and make eye contact with drivers | Step out at once, because cars must stop for you by law | Cross while looking at your phone | Run, because crosswalks are dangerous places to stand and wait at
exercise: A car stops to let you cross, but the road has two lanes. What do you check? | That the cars in the other lane have stopped too | Only that the first car has stopped for you to go | That the driver is smiling and waving at you to go | That the traffic light is red for you and green for them
exercise: What does a school crossing guard or lollipop lady do? | Stops the traffic and signals when children can cross | Teaches children to read traffic signs in class | Gives tickets to drivers who park badly outside the school gates | Helps children find their lost bags
exercise: You drop your pencil case in the middle of the road while crossing. What do you do? | Leave it, finish crossing and get it later when safe | Stop at once and pick it up quickly | Go back to the kerb you came from to think | Kick it ahead of you as you cross
exercise: You are crossing a one-way street. What do you do? | Still look both ways before you cross | Look only the way the cars go, nowhere else | Cross without looking, because it is one-way | Look only at the bus lane and the buses in it
exercise: Why is jaywalking in the USA a bad idea? | It is more dangerous and in many places against the rules | It is allowed everywhere and always safe | Cars drive slower in the middle of a block, so it is safer than a corner | Only adults are allowed to cross at corners
exercise: Which is true about a car that sees you late? | At speed it needs many metres to stop | It can always stop in a moment | Cars stop faster when they go fast | A driver who sees you can always stop in time
test: A girl in Toronto is playing catch near the road and the ball bounces into the street. What should she have done? | Played in a park or yard, away from the road | Thrown the ball more gently on the sidewalk | Run after the ball as quickly as she could, before a car came | Played with a smaller ball
test: A boy in Dublin sees a car stopped at a crossing for him. The lane beside it is busy. Why must he wait? | A car in the next lane may not see him behind the stopped car | The stopped car might change its mind | Only one lane has to stop for him | The rule is to wait for the driver to wave at him twice, then once more
test: Which is true about a car travelling fast? | Even if the driver sees you, they may not stop in time | It can stop at once if the driver brakes hard | Cars stop more quickly when it rains | A fast car is easier to stop than a slow one
test: A girl's hat blows off into the road while she is halfway across. What is the safest thing to do? | Finish crossing and ask an adult to get it when it is safe | Run back to grab it before a car comes | Stand still in the road until a car stops | Chase it, because hats do not cost much and the cars can see a hat clearly
test: Walking to school in Perth, a group of friends meets a man using a white cane on a narrow footpath. What should they do? | Go into single file and step aside so he can pass | Keep walking together; he will hear them coming | Call out to him to move to the edge | Walk faster to get past him first
test: At a school crossing in Sydney there is a lollipop man. When do you cross? | When he has stopped the traffic and signals to you | As soon as you see a gap in the cars | When your friends have already started | When he is looking the other way and busy with the cars on the other side
test: A small boy in Phoenix wants to walk on the road side of his mother. Why should she keep him on the inside? | Small children can dart into the road without warning | Cars like to see children on the road side | It is easier to hold his hand there | Children walk faster on the inside, away from the noise of the cars
test: Why should you not run across a road even if no car is near? | You can trip and cannot react if a car appears | Running across roads is slower than walking | Drivers only stop for people who walk | Running makes cars speed up
test: A boy in Glasgow stands right at the kerb on a rainy day. What is likely to happen? | A passing car may splash him with puddle water | Cars will stop to let him stay dry | The rain will stop sooner for him | Nothing, because cars always steer around puddles near the kerb
test: Which is a good reason not to pat a dog tied up outside a shop? | Alone and tied up, it may be nervous and snap | Dogs never like to be patted by anyone they do not know at all | It will run away if you touch it | Its owner might give you a reward
test: car backing out of the driveway, i'm on the pavement | Stop and let it pass | Run past behind it quickly | Wave and keep walking | Knock on the window to say hello
test: dropped my hat in the middle of the road while crossing! | Leave it, finish crossing, get it later when safe | Run back for it now before a car squashes it | Stop and pick it up | Kick it to the other side
test: one-way street, only need to look one way yeah? | No, still look both ways | Yes, cars only come one way | Don't look, just walk | Only look up the road
test: car stopped for me but the other lane's still going | Check every lane has stopped before you go | Go, one car stopping is enough for all the lanes | Run across fast | Wave to the other cars to stop
explain: A boy in California crossed a street while looking at his phone; he never saw the car turning towards him, and it hit him and broke his leg.
explain: A girl in London ran out between two parked vans after her ball; a cyclist could not stop in time, knocked her down, and she had a broken collarbone.
fact: Children stepping out between parked cars are hidden from drivers until it is too late to stop.
fact: People looking at phones while crossing miss cars they would otherwise have seen.
test: can i text while crossing, it's a small road | No, phone away, look and listen till you're across | Yes, small roads have no cars | Yes, if you walk fast | Yes, if you keep one earbud out
test: ball rolled into the road!! | Let it go and get an adult to fetch it | Run and grab it quickly before a car comes | Kick it back from the kerb | Dash out between the cars
test: Why are children hit so often when they step out between parked cars? | Drivers cannot see them until it is too late | Parked cars make roads wider | Drivers go faster near parked cars | Children are always seen there, as drivers look out for them
test: did u hear a kid was walking across the road on her phone and didn't see the car? real lesson? | Look up and put the phone away when crossing | Phones help you hear traffic | Walk faster while texting so you're in the road for less time | Cars always see people on phones
test: apparently a car stopped for a boy at the crossing, he walked out and a car in the next lane hit him. what's the true lesson? | Check every lane has stopped before passing a stopped car | Run when one car stops | One stopped car means all the cars in every other lane will stop as well | Only trucks are dangerous
test: my mate says on a one-way street u only need to look one way. right? | No, look both ways; bikes and buses may come the other way | Yes, traffic only goes one way | Yes, unless it's raining | No, but only at night, when you can't see the arrows painted on the road
exam: Identify the correct manner of crossing a road. | Stopping, looking and walking, never running | Running so as to cross quickly | Crossing between parked cars | Crossing while looking at a phone, provided one walks quickly
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate response upon dropping something in the road while crossing? | It is left, the crossing finished, and retrieved later | One stops at once to pick it up | One runs back for it immediately, before any vehicle approaches | One waits in the road for a gap
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a car travelling at speed. | It needs many metres to stop even when the driver has seen you | It may stop instantly at any speed | It stops within one metre | It cannot stop until the road ends
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a small child is held on the side away from the traffic. | Small children may dart into the road | Small children walk more slowly | It is required only at night | It keeps the child out of the puddles that collect along the kerb
